  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- The denominator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2-4=-2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{5(ty)^{2}+(tx)(ty)}{2(tx)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}xy}{2{t}^{4}{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(5{y}^{2}+xy)}{{t}^{4}(2{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{{t}^{2}(2{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={t}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={t}^{-2}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-2}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $-2.$",
